---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="gojo"
  description="Schedule, run, and audit autonomous coding agents against your Git repositories — with validation and merge under your control."
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="hero-inner">
      <p class="hero-brand">gojo</p>
      <h1>Run coding agents on a schedule you can trust.</h1>
      <p class="hero-lead">
        Self-hosted orchestration for Cursor, Claude Code, and shell agents — with isolated Git
        worktrees, real validation, and merges the platform owns.
      </p>
      <div class="hero-cta">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/getting-started">Get started</a>
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/first-agent">Your first agent</a>
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/advanced-agent">Advanced agent</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>Built for unattended agent work</h2>
    <p class="section-lead">
      gojo is the operations layer around your agents: schedule the work, isolate the repo, prove
      the result, then integrate it — without letting the agent push straight to main.
    </p>
    <div class="feature-grid">
      <article class="feature">
        <h3>Schedule with intent</h3>
        <p>
          Cron and one-off runs, explicit time zones, overlap policies, retries, and automatic
          disable after repeated failures.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="feature">
        <h3>Isolated Git worktrees</h3>
        <p>
          Every attempt gets its own branch and worktree so concurrent agents never write over each
          other.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="feature">
        <h3>Adapters you already use</h3>
        <p>
          Shell for deterministic scripts; Cursor Agent and Claude Code for coding agents — detected
          from your machine and versioned per run.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="feature">
        <h3>Platform-owned success</h3>
        <p>
          Agents propose work. gojo runs your validation pipeline and decides whether the run
          actually succeeded.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="section">
      <h2>From prompt to pull request</h2>
      <p class="section-lead">
        Define tasks in a repo manifest or the UI. gojo prepares the workspace, runs the agent,
        validates, commits, and integrates according to policy.
      </p>
      <div class="feature-grid">
        <article class="feature">
          <h3>Validation pipelines</h3>
          <p>Install, lint, test, and build steps you define — outside the agent conversation.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="feature">
          <h3>Merge queue</h3>
          <p>
            Commit-only, pull request, await approval, or auto-merge — serialized per project so
            two green runs can’t race into the same branch.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="feature">
          <h3>Handoff reports</h3>
          <p>
            Structured summaries for the next agent or human: what changed, what failed, what to do
            next.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="feature">
          <h3>Full run history</h3>
          <p>Live logs, diffs, artifacts, and an audit trail you can inspect from CLI or web UI.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>Operate locally, stay in control</h2>
    <p class="section-lead">
      One binary (or Bun from source), SQLite on disk, and a web UI that binds to localhost by
      default.
    </p>
    <div class="feature-grid">
      <article class="feature">
        <h3>CLI + web UI</h3>
        <p>
          Install as a systemd or launchd service. Manage projects, runs, and schedules from the
          terminal or the browser.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="feature">
        <h3>Notifications</h3>
        <p>Slack and generic webhooks when runs finish, fail, or schedules disable themselves.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="feature">
        <h3>Crash-safe scheduling</h3>
        <p>
          Leases prevent double-firing. Interrupted runs are recovered instead of silently marked
          successful.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="feature">
        <h3>Secrets & backups</h3>
        <p>
          Encrypted local secret store, scoped access, and backup/restore for config, database, and
          keys.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <div class="hero-cta hero-cta-follow">
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/getting-started">Install gojo</a>
      <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/docs">Documentation</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
